A||N||3||Bob Englert||Removing brake lines||||5||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||05-12-2001||10:53 PM||||I am starting to rebuild the front suspension on one of my 87 GT's that was formerly from Michigan.  The car has a lot of rust and the suspension teardown is going slowly.<P>I also wanted to replace the rubber brake lines to the calipers (they look original).  The problem is the nut on the rigid brake line is starting to round off.  <P>I don't want to break the line or ruin the fitting.  Any suggestions on how to get the rubber line loose at the rigid connection would be appreciated.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||5
Z||000001||ray b||05-12-2001||11:14 PM||||FLARE NUT WRENCH looks like a box wrench with a small bite out of it to fit the over the line. a must have for brake work you only need the one that fits not a whole set.||||reg||1
Z||000002||voyagerspe||05-12-2001||11:45 PM||||This method although it has a slight element of danger is the easyest way to remove you rubber brake lines even if the nut is very frozen to the steel line.  I live in NY and this method always works.<P>1st<BR>    get a pair of vice grips that have the jaws for 6 sided nuts. Any good hardware store should have them. get "vice grip" brand there the best. Also in the package on display at your hardware store it will be griping a nut(this way you know you have the right style of vicegrip).<P>2nd cut the rubber hose (with a pair of snips or wire cutters) as close as you can to the metal coller that connects to the steel brake line.<P>3rd get your propane torch (make sure your car doesnt have a gas leak) Heat the coller untill is cherry red.<P>4th  use the vice grips on the nut and a wrench on the steel line and it will come free.<P><BR>YOU MUST CUT THE RUBBER LINES FIRST.  IF YOU DONT IT WILL BLOW UP IN YOUR FACE.  <BR>also make sure you cut it back as close to the coller as possible. if you leave too much rubber connected to the coller it will blow (it sounds like a m-60) however even if the small amount of rubber does blow (if you have cut the line) there is no hot brake fluid to get you in the face.<P>ok one more time CUT THE LINES BEFORE YOU TORCH THEM .<P>good luck have fun etc.<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||bHooper||05-13-2001||09:55 AM||||hey Bob, i think i have the appropriate flare nut wrench (must as i have swapped brake lines on my car), if you need to borrow one.<P>also, don't forget about my favorite tool in the tool box.  it is most handy in jobs like this.  thats right... the blow torch!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>good for all kinds of problems where you cant bust things free! <P>let me know what i can do.<P>hoop||||reg||1
